A||N||6||jhogans||Waterpump HELP!!||||5||
Z||000000||jhogans||05-03-2001||10:46 PM||||I was quoted approx $600 to replace my waterpump in my 86 GT.<P>How hard would it be to do it myself?  What are the steps?  If I do partake this venture, what else should I replace while I'm doing this?<P>Thanks for any help guys and gals.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||05-03-2001||11:06 PM||||since the water pump is comming off... you may as well replace the timing set. all the instructions, with pictures, are in the haynes or chiltons books.||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||05-04-2001||01:23 AM||||<B>600 bucks!!! DUDE, DONT DO IT!</B> Ive replaced SEVERAL water pumps on my Fieros..all V6's. Its <B>NOT</B><I>that</I>  hard! The pump itself cost around 25 bucks at Autozone. Loosen the alt and take off the belt. The pump is held on by several (about 13 if memory serves) torx head bolts. For whatever reason they vary in size...T-47, T-30...ect. Take the old one off, clean the old gasket material off, and replace. Its kinda time consuming, but not difficult.<BR>600 bucks? Geez! Like Ogre said, just follow the Haynes or Chiltons manual. <BR>p.s. take out the battery..helps for clearance. M7F||||reg||4
Z||000003||rogergarrison||05-04-2001||06:50 AM||||A friend of mine had his replaced at a Pontiac dealer for $350. that was a couple years ago.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Heisenhund||05-04-2001||07:21 AM||||Water pumps are not that difficult. I replaced the one on my 86 GT twice (beware of plastic impellers). Get a Haynes (or Chilton) manual, go to the Online Service Guide, get yourself a water pump at your favorite retailer and spend about 3 hours of good quality time with your Fiero. If you decide to do the timing set, call it 4 hours. Not a big undertaking at all.<P>One thing though, the 86 V6 uses 13mm & 15mm bolts. GM started using Torx bolts on the water pump in 87. So I've heard, all I know is my 85 & 86 GTs use metric bolts.<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||05-04-2001||01:46 PM||||As everyone has stated there are several bolts holding the water pump on the engine (around 11).  There are several sizes and lengths.  I suggest that you take a piece of cardboard and trace the new gasket onto it and poke holes for each bolt. As you remove a bolt clean it up (wire brush the threads) and stick it into the corresponding hole in the cardboard.  That way you won't mix them up.<P>If you are going to replace the timing chain you will need to remove the harmonic damper.  This takes a puller and either a long cheater bar for the socket or an impact wrench.  It also helps to have a long extension for the socket.  Work through the right rear wheel well to remove the damper.  It's not hard to do, just takes a bit of force to get the damper off and on again.  Also change the seal in the timing chain cover if you are doing the chain.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000006||jhogans||05-08-2001||11:11 PM||||Thanks for the info guys.  I will attempt the Waterpump job myself.<P>||||reg||14
